# Sunwave fingerprint sensor: gesture key layout
#
# The fingerprint reader is not just a reader: the `sunwave-fp` driver (on
# spi0.0, even though the device tree node is called `mediatek,goodix-fp` --
# a dual-vendor board) registers an `sf-keys` input device that reports the
# gestures made on the sensor.
#
# Without this file `sf-keys` falls back to Generic.kl, which maps 105 and 106
# as DPAD_LEFT/RIGHT: the swipes would end up moving focus inside apps, which
# makes no sense to anyone unaware the sensor does this too.
#
# The gestures are NOT on by default: the HAL reports them only with
# <navigation>true</navigation> in its sw_config.xml, otherwise every touch,
# swipes included, comes as the tap. The stock file leaves it off; ours is
# mounted over it at boot, see rootdir/etc/init/s88pro-fingerprint.rc.
#
# What was actually observed, with `getevent -lt` while using the sensor (the
# counts come from a 45-second session):
#
#   HAL "nav Key" -> SF_KEY_F11   -> scancode  68  KEY_F10    162 eventi
#   HAL "nav R"   -> SF_KEY_RIGHT -> scancode 106  KEY_RIGHT   34 eventi
#   HAL "nav L"   -> SF_KEY_LEFT  -> scancode 105  KEY_LEFT    28 eventi
#
# The sensor resolves one axis only: swipes along the phone's long axis are
# classified as taps, not as a direction.
#
# The two swipes become AOSP's fingerprint navigation keys, which open and
# close the notification shade once the user turns on Settings > System >
# Gestures > Swipe fingerprint for notifications (config_supportSystemNavigationKeys
# in the framework overlay). One swipe opens, the other closes; which is which
# was chosen on the phone.
key 105   SYSTEM_NAVIGATION_DOWN
key 106   SYSTEM_NAVIGATION_UP

# The tap is left unmapped. It comes with every touch of the sensor, unlocking
# included, and no setting in Android gives it a meaning; unmapped, it reaches
# apps as an unknown key, which they ignore.
#
# Declared by the driver in the sf-keys bitmap but never observed coming out.
key 143   WAKEUP    WAKE
